  "judge_reasoning": "# Evaluation Analysis\n\n## Task Breakdown\nThe instruction has two components:\n1. **Determine** whether Non-English submissions to arXiv require a multi-language abstract\n2. **If required**, identify the separator used between the multiple abstracts\n\n## Screenshot Evidence\n\n**Screenshot 1-2**: Navigation from arXiv homepage to Submission Guidelines, then to the \"Non-English submissions\" policy page — establishing the research path.\n\n**Screenshot 3**: The current policy page \"Submissions in Languages Other Than English and Multiple-Language Submissions\" confirms non-English submissions are accepted but require an English version of the paper.\n\n**Screenshots 4-5** (Wayback capture of info.arxiv.org/help/faq/multilang.html): This is the critical evidence, showing the \"Preparation of a multilingual abstract\" section which explicitly states:\n- *\"We accept submissions in languages other than English but do require that all submissions have an English version of the abstract.\"*\n- The example code block shows the format:\n  ```\n  English version of abstract for your important paper.\n  \n  -----\n  \n  Other language version of abstract for your important paper.\n  ```\n- The text explicitly notes: *\"Note the blank lines before and after the separator (-----)\"*\n\n## Verification of Response\n\nThe Result Response states:\n1. ✅ **Yes**, a multi-language abstract is required (English abstract in addition to the original language abstract) — this matches the screenshot content exactly.\n2. ✅ The separator is **a line of five hyphens: `-----`**, with blank lines before and after — this matches the code block shown in screenshots 4 and 5.\n3. The response also correctly cites the source (info.arxiv.org/help/faq/multilang.html) and adds the current policy note about the full English paper requirement (visible in screenshot 3).\n\nBoth parts of the question are accurately answered, and the answer is fully corroborated by the screenshots.\n\n## Verdict\n\n**SUCCESS**",
